So, $10 < n^2 < 1000$.
This implies $\sqrt{10} < n < \sqrt{1000}$.
$3.16... < n < 31.62...$
The integers $n$ are $4, 5, ..., 31$.
We need odd integers $n$.
The odd integers are $5, 7, 9, ..., 31$.
The number of terms is $\frac{31-5}{2} + 1 = \frac{26}{2} + 1 = 13 + 1 = 14$.
The squares are $25, 49, 81, 121, 169, 225, 289, 361, 441, 529, 625, 729, 841, 961$.
All these squares are odd and are between 10 and 1000.
The count is indeed 14.
The choice is (c).
